recommended_fix:
Remove the commented-out `SET XACT_ABORT ON` and make the atomic behaviour explicit in Snowflake Scripting: wrap the body of the procedure in `BEGIN TRANSACTION; ... COMMIT;` and add an `EXCEPTION WHEN OTHER THEN ROLLBACK; RAISE;` handler, which reproduces the abort-and-roll-back-everything semantics that `XACT_ABORT ON` provided. For `etl.usp_Load_SalesDW`, replace the T-SQL `BEGIN TRY / BEGIN CATCH ... IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK` pattern with:

```sql
BEGIN
  BEGIN TRANSACTION;
  -- load steps: CALL etl.usp_Load_DimDate(...), etl.usp_Load_DimProduct(...), etc.
  COMMIT;
EXCEPTION
  WHEN OTHER THEN
    ROLLBACK;
    CALL etl.usp_LogStep(:run_id, :step, 'Failed', NULL, SQLERRM);
    RAISE;
END;
```

Use `ROLLBACK` unconditionally in the handler (Snowflake has no `@@TRANCOUNT`; if a transaction check is needed, read `CURRENT_TRANSACTION()` and roll back only when it is not NULL). Note that nested `BEGIN TRANSACTION` calls in called procedures are flattened into the single outermost transaction in Snowflake, so keep transaction control in this orchestrating procedure and let the child load procedures run without their own COMMIT. Where a step must be logged even after a failure, perform the logging call after `ROLLBACK` so the audit insert is not discarded with the rolled-back work.
